# tools/ales_stereo_limit_witness.rish — stereo_limit: the brickwall limiter carried into stereo, the ratio→∞ corner of the linked gain — both a true ceiling and an image held whole (ALES158).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES158.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-063222_fill-ales158-lotus-stereo-limit.md
# Stands on: lotus/limit.rye (ALES49 — the mono brickwall limiter, its limit and LimitError reused whole for the peak-channel proof and the fault set), lotus/stereo_compress.rye (ALES157 — the linked-gain sibling whose
# ratio→∞ corner this rung is), lotus/stereo_gate.rye (ALES156 — the rung that opened the stereo dynamics family with the linked detector), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip and its balance invariant), lotus/timeline.rye
# (ALES2 — the Clip, sample_max).
#
# lotus/stereo_limit.rye limits a span of sample-PAIRS in a master to a magnitude ceiling, with ONE linked gain across the two channels — the ratio→∞ corner of ALES157's compressor. The detector key per pair is
# max(|left|, |right|): within the ceiling the pair passes byte for byte; above it BOTH channels are scaled by the single fraction ceil/key, so the peak-holding channel lands exactly on ceil and the quieter one is scaled by
# the identical fraction. The linked form is simultaneously a true brickwall (no written sample exceeds the ceiling) AND image-preserving (both channels share one gain), where the independent mono lift gives only the first.
# LimitError reused whole; the linked lift adds no fault. Values only, never a length. The crux is the brickwall + linked-gain law (no sample exceeds ceil, the peak channel equals the mono limit byte for byte, the quiet
# channel scaled by the same fraction, the independent lift genuinely differing), the image law (the shared gain preserves the L:R ratio, an out-of-phase over-ceiling pair returning still out of phase), the sub-ceiling law
# (a pair within the linked ceiling passing byte for byte), and the ceiling-at-max law (a ceiling at sample_max the identity for all in-range audio).
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_stereo_limit_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-limit: Language EN — stereo_limit limits a span of sample-pairs in a stereo master to a magnitude ceiling, with one linked gain across the two channels. For each pair the detector key is the max of the two channels' magnitudes, the louder of the two: within the ceiling the pair passes byte for byte; above it both channels are scaled by the single fraction ceil over key, so the peak-holding channel lands exactly on the ceiling and the quieter channel is scaled by the identical fraction. The linked form is at once a true brickwall (no written sample exceeds the ceiling) and image-preserving (both channels share one gain, so the left-to-right ratio holds). It validates the ceiling and span once against the shared length before any write, so a refusal never limits one channel and leaves the other torn. It writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced; an empty span is a clean no-op on both."
say "ales-stereo-limit: Style Radiant — ALES49 named the mono limiter: a hard ceiling the signal may touch yet never cross — a sample within the ceiling passes byte for byte, a sample louder is pinned to sign(x) times the ceiling, the honest floor of the whole dynamics family. Carried into stereo, this rung is the compressor's linked gain, ALES157, taken to its limit: the ratio to infinity corner already foreshadowed by that rung's seat-at-threshold knob law. Lifting the mono limiter per channel would flatten an asymmetric transient on the loud channel while the quiet passes, shifting the stereo image, so this rung takes ALES157's shape to its limit: one gain from the linked detector, applied to both. The quiet beauty is that the linked form is simultaneously a true brickwall, since after the call no sample in either channel exceeds the ceiling as the peak lands on the ceiling and everything else is scaled below it, and image-preserving, since both channels share one gain and the left-to-right ratio holds. The independent mono limiter gives only the first. The crux is five laws: the brickwall and linked-gain law (no sample exceeds the ceiling, and the peak-holding channel equals the mono limit byte for byte while both channels are scaled by the same fraction, the independent lift genuinely differing); the image law (the shared gain preserves the left-to-right ratio, an out-of-phase over-ceiling pair returning still out of phase, no magnitude expanding and no sign flipping); the sub-ceiling law (a pair within the linked ceiling passing byte for byte on both); the ceiling-at-max law (a ceiling at sample_max the identity for all in-range audio); and the balance, atomicity, and degenerate law (length held, a zero or over-rail ceiling and an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, count zero and the empty span the identity on both)."
say "ales-stereo-limit: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) limited by one linked gain, one comparison of the linked key and at most one multiply-then-divide per channel per sample (in i64, the product of a magnitude and the ceiling at most 32767 times 32767 never overflowing), memoryless (no attack or release time base; that is a later rung); the two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (a limiter writes values only, each length asserted equal and within bound on exit); the ceiling and span are validated once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never limits one channel and leaves the other torn; StereoLimitError is ALES49's LimitError (BadCeiling, BadRange) reused whole, the linked lift adding no fault. Nothing on the audio path can overflow: on the scaled path the ceiling is below the key, so every scaled magnitude is at most its input's and at most the ceiling, which fits i16, and the sign is carried by construction. The brickwall and linked-gain law is proven against a master where the left always holds the peak — no sample crossing the ceiling on either channel, the left equal to the mono limit of it byte for byte (8000 to 1000, 4000 to 1000, 500 within, 2000 to 1000) while the right is scaled by the same fraction (2000 to 250, 1000 to 250, 100 within, 1500 to 750), and the independent lift (mono limit of the right alone, 2000 to 1000) shown to genuinely differ; the image law proves no magnitude expands and no sign flips over a mixed master and that an out-of-phase over-ceiling pair returns still exactly out of phase (both landing on the ceiling with opposite sign); the sub-ceiling law proves a master within the linked ceiling passes byte for byte on both; the ceiling-at-max law proves a ceiling at sample_max the identity for all in-range audio; and the balance, atomicity, and degenerate cases prove the length held, each of the three refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the ceiling a magnitude in sample units, no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_limit"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_limit.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-limit: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_limit.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-limit: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-limit: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-limit' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-limit: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-limit line"
